# Issue, branch, and pull request

Every code change in this project follows all three steps. Do not commit straight to `main`. Do not force-push `main`.

1. An issue exists first. If the user did not give an issue number, create one before writing code.
2. Do the work on a feature branch, for example `feat/16-date-range-tuple`. Never commit the change directly on `main`.
3. Open a pull request against `main` when the work is ready, and link the issue.

```text
# Bad
git checkout main
git commit -m "feat: add the date range"
git push origin main

# Good
# issue #16 already exists
git checkout -b feat/16-date-range-tuple
git commit -m "feat: store a date range as an ordered pair"
git push -u origin HEAD
gh pr create --base main
```
